PQC migration (whitepaper 1.1): the NIP-44 v2 conversation-key KDF
gets its 32-byte shared secret from ML-KEM-768 instead of ECDH. The
wire format carries the new pqc2: prefix so a relay that only
knows v1 can refuse to forward cleanly.

- src/security/nip44-v2.ts:
  - deriveConversationKey runs HKDF-SHA256 over the 32-byte shared
    secret and returns the 76-byte OKM (32 hmac + 32 chacha + 12 nonce).
  - pad / unpad implement the precise NIP-44 v2 padding scheme: a
    2-byte big-endian length prefix, then the data zero-padded to the
    next multiple of 32, with a 32-byte minimum. unpad enforces the
    exact `padded.length % 32 === 0` modulus and the length range
    before the structural sanity check.
  - ChaCha20 encryption uses the IETF variant via Node's
    chacha20 cipher with a synthesised 16-byte IV (4-byte counter=0
    + 12-byte nonce), matching RFC 8439 §2.4.
  - HMAC-SHA256 MAC binds the ML-KEM ciphertext and the ChaCha20
    ciphertext together; verification uses constant-time compare.
  - encryptNip44V2 / decryptNip44V2 round-trip through the pqc2: wire
    format. isPqcNip44Envelope auto-detects the prefix.
  - All length and structural checks fail closed with a typed
    Nip44V2Error that is safe to catch in higher-level handlers.
- src/security/nip44-v2.test.ts: 18 invariants - canonical constants,
  prefix auto-detect, OKM length + 32-byte shared-secret guard,
  padding on 1/30/33-byte plaintexts and round-trip, length-field
  range and structural checks, ChaCha20 round-trip, ML-KEM-768
  ciphertext length validation, pqc2: format detection, MAC failure
  on each field of the wire (kem ct / chacha ct / mac), wrong-shape
  envelope rejection, and a tampered-ciphertext integration check.

